WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:05.120
The Nun is a big step forward for The Conjuring
series, giving the shared universe of horror

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.280
movies its biggest opening weekend yet.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.000
The third franchise sub-series after The Conjuring
and Annabelle movies, The Nun works harder

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.500
than any of its predecessors to make connections
to the films that came before it.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:20.080
Whether it's a visual reference to a past
movie, the conclusion of a series-long plot,

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:24.320
or the presence of the demon Valak's name
all over the place, the movie has plenty to

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.780
offer for eagle-eyed viewers looking for clues
about how everything connects.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.640
If you watched the spooky movie through your
fingers, we've got you covered - here are

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.920
some small details you might've missed in
The Nun.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.460
Original creation

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:40.050
The Nun is the fifth entry in the expanding
Conjuring universe, and it asserts itself

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.710
within that continuity in a variety of ways.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:46.780
For one, the movie's opening scene about two
sisters in the St. Cartas Abbey fighting off

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:52.010
the demon nun features an almost-perfect recreation
of the post-credits scene of Annabelle: Creation,

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.920
which teased the demon nun spinoff in 2017.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.180
In The Nun, it's revealed that the spooky
spectre coming down the hall is pursuing Sister

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:03.579
Victoria, who sacrifices herself to keep the
demon from possessing her and entering the

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:04.180
real world.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.820
Curiously, The Nun lacks a similar post-credits
sequence of its own.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.680
For whatever reason, it looks like the series'
producers don't want to give people any hints

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.200
of what's to come before Conjuring 3, Annabelle
3, or their next spinoff, The Crooked Man.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:17.760
Demonic DJ

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.031
After Father Burke and Sister Irene arrive
at the abbey for an overnight stay, their

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.940
sleep is interrupted by Valak, which proves
itself to be a real nuisance of a demon.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.400
Instead of killing everyone immediately, the
monster's first move is to wake Burke up in

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.280
the middle of the night by blaring the abbey
radio with an on-the-nose song choice.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.500
Specifically, the radio that interrupts Burke's
slumber is playing Jo Stafford's version of

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.380
the tune "You Belong to Me," which means exactly
what it sounds like it means.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.040
Real subtle, Valak.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:45.400
Precious memories

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.000
Visible in the background of Irene and Burke's
first dinner in the abbey is a photograph

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.860
that will be familiar to anyone who watched
Annabelle: Creation.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.840
The black-and-white snapshot was originally
referenced by Sister Charlotte in the demon

00:01:56.860 --> 00:02:02.100
doll story, which chronologically takes place
in 1955, three years after the events of The

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:02.600
Nun.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.880
The photo shows Sister Charlotte alongside
three nuns of the St. Cartas Abbey, whom she

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.540
says she became very close to at some point
in the past.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:13.960
Those relationships aren't built on in The
Nun though, since by the time Irene and Burke

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:18.880
arrive at the abbey in 1952, every one of
the nuns Charlotte knew is presumably dead.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:22.780
Considering what we learn about the abbey,
it's almost a little jarring to see the Sisters

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.460
there ever having a moment of levity.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.180
Does Charlotte in 1955 even know her friends
are dead, or that they gave their lives for

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.180
such important work?

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:31.680
It doesn't seem that way.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.980
She doesn't even recognize the face of Valak
that haunts her copy of the photograph, and

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.000
seems to have no idea how lucky she is to
have made it out alive.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.360
What's your name?

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.519
Supernatural activity is at work in The Nun
before Burke and Irene ever arrive at the

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:45.020
abbey.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.180
If you look closely, you can see the demon's
name pop up in the world around them - a strange

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.640
phenomenon that seems to surround the demon's
activity.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.279
As Burke and Irene prepare to embark for the
abbey with their local guide Maurice, Burke

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.560
accidentally places his bags in the back of
an unrelated truck.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:04.040
It's a humorous moment, except for one odd
thing: the vehicle's license plate.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.940
Aside from a few junk letters and numbers
breaking up the sequence, the plate for the

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.220
most part reads "VALAK."

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.629
The demon's name can also be partially seen
in Irene's introductory scene, spelled out

00:03:13.629 --> 00:03:16.480
backwards and vertically on the wall of her
school classroom.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:19.080
Valak's presence did the same thing in The
Conjuring 2.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:24.200
It's a weird and unexplained phenomenon, considering
that the demon's name is its biggest weakness.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.480
For some reason or another, Valak can't stop
spilling the beans.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.100
Pazuzu style

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:33.220
Like a lot of demon-centric horror movies,
The Nun owes a lot to The Exorcist.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:37.560
At least one frightening image in the movie
is a very clear visual reference to that 70s

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:38.060
classic.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.800
As Father Burke lays trapped in a wooden coffin,
buried alive in the abbey's graveyard, he

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.360
is grabbed from behind by Valak.

00:03:44.589 --> 00:03:48.999
As the demon claws at Burke's face, the audience
can briefly see the twisted visage of the

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.900
demon flash within the darkness beside Burke.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:55.650
The moment is highly reminiscent of The Exorcist,
with that movie's demon appearing in snarling,

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.160
near-subliminal flash frames.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:01.430
In both movies, the quick glimpses at the
faces of the demons are blink-and-you'll-miss-it

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:04.080
moments, making them some of the scariest
parts of either one.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:05.620
Conjuring a connection

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:10.799
The Nun brings its story full circle in its
final minutes, cutting from Romania in 1952

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.960
to Wakefield, Massachusetts, 20 years later.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.000
This postscript scene loops the finale of
The Nun back into the very first Conjuring

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:21.160
movie, revealing that the lecture given by
Ed and Lorraine Warren in that film was all

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:25.120
along referring to an encounter the couple
had with Maurice, aka "Frenchie."

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.220
Just before the epilogue kicks in, Maurice
is revealed to have been possessed by Valak

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.240
during the final battle in the abbey's catacombs.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.520
It makes for a pretty sad end to the otherwise
charming tour guide.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:36.440
"So what happened to Maurice?"

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.800
"Well he tried… gun on himself."

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.290
The ending of The Nun briefly recreates The
Conjuring's scene, using brand new footage

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:49.360
of The Nun's Maurice, played by Jonas Bloquet
in place of the original actor.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:53.840
It's a moment that solidifies The Nun's connection
to the first two Conjuring movies, revealing

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:57.620
that the Warrens' visions of the demon nun
were implanted into them by their encounter

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:58.760
with the possessed Maurice.

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:05.160
What began in Romania in 1952 ultimately comes
to an end in 1977 London, when the Warrens

00:05:05.161 --> 00:05:09.410
decisively banish Valak from the earthly realm
- for now, at least.

